# 📖 Steve's Journal

## ✦ Chapter 6 — The Compass

> "There is nothing more frustrating than knowing where you want to go and having no idea which way you're facing."
>
> — Steve

---

## ✦ The Problem

I have always enjoyed exploring.  There is something wonderful about leaving the safety of home and discovering places that have never been seen before. New forests. Deep caves. Mountains that seem to reach the clouds. Ancient structures hidden beneath the earth.  But exploration has one rather annoying problem.

Getting lost.

It happens more often than I care to admit. I set out with a destination in mind, travel for what feels like hours, and eventually discover that I have been walking in entirely the wrong direction.  I carry a compass, of course. Everyone does. But checking it means stopping, looking down, and trying to work out which direction I am facing.  It doesn't sound like much trouble.  Until you are crossing a mountain, running from monsters, or trying to find your way through a dense forest.

There must be a better way.

---

## ✦ The Experiment

I began studying my compass more closely.  It is a clever little device. No matter where I travel, it seems to know which way to point. But I wondered if there was a way to make that knowledge more useful.  I wanted to know which direction I was facing without having to stop and examine the compass every few moments.  My first experiments were rather disappointing.  I tried attaching the compass to different tools. I tried enchanting it with various directional spells. I even attempted to create a larger compass that would display several directions at once.  Most of the attempts did absolutely nothing.  One experiment made the needle spin so wildly that I had to put the compass away before it made me dizzy.  Still, I was beginning to understand something.

The compass already knew the direction. I simply needed a way to make that information easier to see.

---

## ✦ The Discovery

The breakthrough came while I was walking through a forest.  I had been following a narrow path when I stopped to examine a strange formation in the distance. When I turned back toward the trail, I realized I had completely lost track of which way I had been walking.  I reached for my compass.  Then I noticed something.  The moment I turned, the needle changed. It was constantly responding to my movement.  It wasn't just pointing somewhere. It was telling me something about my own direction.  I returned to the workshop and began experimenting again.  This time, instead of trying to change the compass, I tried connecting its directional magic to a simple display.  The first attempt showed nothing useful. The second displayed a direction, but it was difficult to read. The third was better.

Then it finally worked.

A small directional display appeared before me. As I turned, the direction changed with me. North. Northeast. East. Southeast. South. Southwest. West. Northwest.  Eight directions. Always visible. No need to stop and inspect the compass.  I stood there turning in circles for longer than I care to admit.

It was beautiful.

---

## ✦ A Better Way to Explore

I immediately took the discovery outside.  I walked through the forest, climbed a nearby hill, and crossed the open plains. Wherever I turned, the display followed. For the first time, I could concentrate on the world around me instead of constantly checking which way I was facing.  I could keep moving and still know my direction.  I even tested it at night.  The results were just as good.  I began thinking about all the explorers who had struggled with the same problem. All the times I had wandered away from home, lost track of a trail, or spent half a day trying to find a familiar landmark.  Perhaps this little discovery could save someone else the trouble.

---

## ✦ The Compass

I have decided to call my discovery the Compass.  It may seem like a small improvement, but sometimes the simplest discoveries are the most useful.  The compass does not tell me where to go. It does not choose my destination or decide which path I should take.  It simply helps me understand which way I am facing.  And sometimes, that is all I need.  There are still countless places I have yet to explore. Mountains I have not climbed. Caves I have not entered. Lands I have not mapped.  But now, wherever I travel, I will always know which direction I am heading.

> "The world is far too big to spend your time walking in circles."

---

## ✦ A Note About My Work

My directional discovery has become something that other explorers can use. I have preserved the method so that anyone can keep track of their direction without constantly stopping to check a compass.If you have places to discover and roads yet to travel, perhaps this little invention will make your journeys easier.

### 📖 Acquire My Compass

Never lose your sense of direction again.

